@page  calling_sequence  Calling Sequence



@section  sec_calling_sequence_intro  Introduction.

Plugins are dynamic libraries (bundles on macosx, DSOs on linux, DLLs on windows).

They should define and export the following entry points.

@ref  PI_Open() and PI_Close() are mandatory, although most often you need more than those two.



@section  sec_global_lifetime  The overall calling sequence for all plugins.

When TVPaint launches, it calls PI_Open() on every plugin, and when it shutdowns it calls PI_Close().

Inbetween these two calls, any of PI_About(), PI_Parameters() and PI_Msg() can be called any number of times in any order.


@subsection  sub_PI_Open  @ref  int  PI_Open( PIFilter*  iPlugin );
When TVPaint (or another host implementing this SDK) loads a plugin, it calls PI_Open().
This function is responsible for filling in the following fields :
- PIVersion, PIRevision: version/revision of the plugin
- PIName: name of the plugin (which will appear in the 'Filter' menu)

Usually this function also opens the file containing localized messages (you need it to fill in the PIName field of PIFilter).

It should also open the plugin's window if it was open the last time TVPaint was shut down.


@subsection  sub_PI_About  @ref  void  PI_About( PIFilter*  iPlugin );
This will be called when the user asks for some information about the plugin.
It can simply not exists and nothing bad will happen.
It can be as simple as a call to TVWarning with an info string,
or as complex as a whole new window with animations, etc... its up to you (and your ego :) ). 

@subsection  sub_PI_Parameters  @ref int  PI_Parameters( PIFilter*  iPlugin, const char*  iArgs );
PI_Parameters() can be called in two (three actually...) ways :
- iArgs is NULL : the plugin has been selected in the "Windows > Plugins" main menu. It should open a window for the user to interact with.
- iArgs is non-NULL : iArgs contains a 'parameter string', which will be interpreted by the plugin in its own way.\n
This happens in two cases :
    - through a call to "tv_filter" in a George script (or the call TVSendCmd() in another plugin).
    - the user selected an alias of your filter in the "Windows > Plugins" menu (aliases are explained in TVCreateAlias()).
The plugin should do the computation right now by initializing whatever data it needs and calling TVExecute().


@subsection  sub_PI_Msg  @ref  int  PI_Msg( PIFilter*  iPlugin, INTPTR  iEvent, INTPTR  iReq, INTPTR*  iArgs );
PI_Msg() is called by TVPaint whenever something of interest to the plugin happens :
- PICMETA_\* messages are always sent to this function.
- PICBREQ_\* messages are only sent to this function if the window to which they belong was created without a specific function to handle messages.


@subsection  sub_PI_Close  @ref  void  PI_Close( PIFilter*  iPlugin );
When TVPaint shuts down, it calls PI_Close().

In this function you should do some cleanup, like closing the locale-file, freeing all the memory you allocated (don't forget the keys !).



@section  sec_applying_a_filter  When applying a filter.

When the plugin calls TVExecute(), TVPaint will prepare the execution of a filter, then calls the following functions, in this order, in your plugin :
- PI_SequenceStart() with the total number of frames to be processed (depending on the selection the user has done in TVPaint).
- For each of these frames, it will then call :
    - PI_Start()
    - PI_Work()
    - PI_Finish()
- PI_SequenceFinish().

@subsection  sub_PI_SequenceStart  @ref  int  PI_SequenceStart( PIFilter*  iPlugin, int  iCount );

Here you allocate and/or initialize everything that is constant throughout all frames.

@subsection  sub_PI_Start  @ref  int  PI_Start( PIFilter*  iPlugin, double  iPos, double  iSize );

Here, you allocate and/or initialize data for processing this frame.
@todo //TODO: try to explain why iPos and iSize are doubles !?!?!?!??!?!

@subsection  sub_PI_Work  @ref  int  PI_Work( PIFilter*  iPlugin );

Here, you actually modify the image (and you DON'T in ANY of the other functions !).

@subsection  sub_PI_Finish  @ref  void  PI_Finish( PIFilter*  iPlugin );

Here, you clean up what you've done in PI_Start().

@subsection  sub_PI_SequenceFinish  @ref  void  PI_SequenceFinish( PIFilter*  iPlugin );

Here, you clean up what you've done in PI_SequenceStart().


*/
